.TH diff3 "" "Command"
.SH NAME
diff3 \- summarize differences among three files
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBdiff3 [\-e|\-x|\-3] \fIgenerator\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
diff3 -- summarize differences among three files
Usage:	diff3 [ -ex3 ] file1 file2 file3
Options:
	-e	Editor script to make changes between 'file2' and 'file3'
		to 'file1' (changes marked with '====' or '====3')
	-x	Above script with only changes marked '====' (all different)
	-3	Above script with changes marked '====3' (file3 different)
.HE
.B diff3
collates two sets of line-oriented differences -- one between
.I file1
and
.IR file3 ,
the other between
.I file2
and
.I file3
-- into one three-way comparison, and either prints a summary of
where the three files disagree, or, with
.BR \-e ,
.BR \-x ,
or
.BR \-3 ,
an editor script.
.PP
The two input difference files must already exist, one giving the
changes from
.I file1
to
.I file3
and the other the changes from
.I file2
to
.IR file3 ,
in the same range-and-letter notation that
.B "diff \-e"
produces.
As shipped in this source tree,
.B diff3
is only the back end that reads those two difference files and
collates them; it takes exactly one argument,
.IR generator ,
and reads
.IB generator a
and
.IB generator b
as the file1-versus-file3 and file2-versus-file3 difference files,
respectively.
Producing those two files (normally with two runs of
.B "diff \-e"
into temporary files named
.IB generator a
and
\fIgenerator\fBb\fR)
is the job of a front-end script; no such front end is present in
this tree (see NOTES).
.PP
Without
.BR \-e ,
.BR \-x ,
or
.BR \-3 ,
.B diff3
prints a summary in which each region of disagreement is headed by
one of:
.IP \fB====\fR 0.6i
All three files differ in this region.
.IP "\fB====\fI1\fR"
Only
.I file1
differs (its text is not reflected in the region of
.IR file3 ).
.IP "\fB====\fI2\fR"
Only
.I file2
differs.
.IP "\fB====\fI3\fR"
Only
.I file3
differs.
.PP
Each header is followed by a line for each of the (up to three)
files that differ in that region, giving the file number, and, in
.B ed
notation, the range of lines and whether they were appended (\fBa\fR)
or changed (\fBc\fR), followed by the affected text itself.
.IP \fB\-e\fR
Instead of the summary, print an
.B ed
script that, applied to
.IR file1 ,
carries forward every change that was made getting from
.I file2
to
.I file3
(that is, every region marked
.B ====
or
.BR ====3 ).
.IP \fB\-x\fR
Like
.BR \-e ,
but the script carries forward only the regions marked
.B ====
(where all three files differ).
.IP \fB\-3\fR
Like
.BR \-e ,
but the script carries forward only the regions marked
.B ====3
(where only
.I file3
differs).
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B diff3
exits with status 0 if the collated differences were empty (the
inputs describe no changes), 1 if they were not, and 2 if it could
not open one of
.IB generator a
or
\fIgenerator\fBb\fR,
or was given a bad argument.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B diff
.SH NOTES
The header comment in this program's source describes it explicitly
as ``the internal part of diff3'', meant to reside at
.B /usr/lib/diff3
and be invoked as
.B "/usr/lib/diff3 [options] generator"
after something else has run
.B "diff \-e"
twice to create
.IB generator a
and
\fIgenerator\fBb\fR.
This source tree installs the identical binary at both
.B /bin/diff3
and
.BR /usr/lib/diff3 ,
but does not contain the front-end script (traditionally also called
.BR diff3 )
that would create the two intermediate difference files and invoke
this program with a temporary-file generator name.
Consequently, running
.B /bin/diff3
with three file names, as the option summary above and the classic
.B diff3
command line suggest, does not work in this tree as built; it must be
invoked with a single
.I generator
argument naming an already-prepared pair of
.B "diff \-e"
output files.
